Q: Interleave bits efficiently A: <p>Since C++29, the easiest possible way is to use <a href="https://cppreference.com/cpp/numeric/bit_expand" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::bit_expand</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;bit&gt;
std::uint64_t interleave(std::uint32_t hi, std::uint32_t lo) {
    // hi mask is 101010..., lo mask is 010101...
    return std::bit_expand(std::uint64_t{hi}, std::uint64_t{0xAAAA'AAAA'AAAA'AAAA})
         | std::bit_expand(std::uint64_t{lo}, std::uint64_t{0x5555'5555'5555'5555});
}
</code></pre>
<p>When available in hardware, <code>std::bit_expand</code> translates to the underlying <code>pdep</code> instruction in hardware, which has only 3 cycles latency and throughput of 1 per clock or better in modern CPUs.  (Or on non-x86 CPUs, some equivalent if available, such as AArch64 copying to an SVE vector register for <code>bdep</code>.)</p>
<p>Unfortunately, no standard library implements this function yet at the time of writing; it's brand-new.</p>
<p>Surprisingly, <code>std::bit_expand</code> isn't x86 <code>pext</code>.<br />
<a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/pdep" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PDEP</a> / <a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/pext" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PEXT</a> stand for Parallel Bits Deposit / Extract, not <em>expand</em>.  <code>pdep</code> takes low bits and puts them at the mask position.  <code>pext</code> extracts the bits selected by the mask and packs them into the bottom <code>popcnt(mask)</code> positions of the output (<code>std::bit_compress</code>).</p>
<p>The equivalent x86 AVX-512 operations on whole vector elements according to a mask (rather than on bits within a single integer) are called <code>VPCOMPRESSB/W/D/Q</code> and <code>VPEXPANDB/W/D/Q</code>, so do match the C++ compress/expand naming convention.</p>

Q: How to pass data from the Angular UI into a parent model and child model using the same model or a single model A: <p>The best practice is usually <strong>not to use your Entity/Domain model directly as your Angular form model</strong>, especially when you have parent-child relationships and optional properties.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Suppose your API model looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>export interface Order {
    id?: number;
    orderNumber?: string;
    case?: Case;
}

export interface Case {
    referenceNumber?: string;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Because <code>case</code> is optional, Angular Signal Forms correctly infer that:</p>
<pre><code>OrderForm.case
</code></pre>
<p>may be <code>undefined</code>.</p>
<p>This leads to template errors such as:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;input [formField]=&quot;OrderForm.case.referenceNumber&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>because TypeScript cannot guarantee that <code>case</code> exists.</p>
<h3>Recommended Solution</h3>
<p>Create a dedicated form model where all properties required by the UI are always present:</p>
<pre><code>export interface OrderFormModel {
    orderNumber: string;
    case: {
        referenceNumber: string;
    };
}
</code></pre>
<p>Initialize the signal with valid defaults:</p>
<pre><code>orderModel = signal&lt;OrderFormModel&gt;({
    orderNumber: '',
    case: {
        referenceNumber: ''
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Create mapper functions between API models and form models:</p>
<pre><code>function mapToFormModel(order: Order): OrderFormModel {
    return {
        orderNumber: order.orderNumber ?? '',
        case: {
            referenceNumber:
                order.case?.referenceNumber ?? ''
        }
    };
}
</code></pre>
<p>When data arrives from the server:</p>
<pre><code>this.orderForm().value.set(
    mapToFormModel(order)
);
</code></pre>
<p>Now the template is fully type-safe:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;input [formField]=&quot;orderForm.case.referenceNumber&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>No non-null assertions (<code>!</code>) are required.</p>
<h3>Why this is preferred</h3>
<p>Your API/domain model and your form model have different responsibilities:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Domain/API model</strong> represents what the server can send (<code>undefined</code> values are valid).</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Form model</strong> represents what the UI needs to render (controls should always exist).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Keeping them separate provides:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Strong typing</p>
</li>
<li><p>No <code>!</code> operators</p>
</li>
<li><p>Cleaner templates</p>
</li>
<li><p>Easier validation</p>
</li>
<li><p>Better maintainability as forms grow</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Regarding Parent and Child Models</h3>
<p>If the UI edits both the parent and child data on the same screen and saves them together, use a <strong>single composite DTO</strong> for the API request:</p>
<pre><code>export interface OrderSaveDto {
    orderNumber: string;
    case: {
        referenceNumber: string;
    };
}
</code></pre>
<p>Submit the entire object graph in a single request and let the backend map the DTO to the parent and child entities.</p>
<p>This is generally preferable to posting separate parent and child models unless they are edited and saved independently.</p>
